  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 143 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nSlightly \"cooler\" today thanks to a shallow backdoor front, with\nmost of the plains sitting at mid-to-high 70s early this\nafternoon. We have been slow to warm-up today as recent ACARS\nsoundings still showing an inversion in place. For this reason,\nhave lowered daytime temperatures a bit, but still expect low 80s\nas the high temperature for today. Current radar imagery shows\nshowers popping up over the high terrain. Expect isolated showers,\nand possibly a few rumbles of thunder, through early evening with\nlittle precipitation reaching the ground. While convection should\nstay confined to the high terrain, can't rule out a virga shower\nspilling onto the I-25 Corridor and Palmer Divide. If this occurs,\ngusty outflow winds of 30-35 mph is possible. \n\nWarm and mostly dry conditions will persist through the end of the\nweek and into the weekend as the upper level ridge resides over\nthe southwestern U.S. High temperatures will continue to be 10-15\ndegrees above normal, reaching the mid 80s through Saturday. We\nmight be able to tie our record temperature for October 8 \n(record:\n85 set in 2020, forecast: 84). Like the past few days, any\nprecipitation chances will be confined to the mountains. \n\nBy late Friday/early Saturday, flow aloft will increase and turn\nmore southwesterly ahead of a system out west. With this increased\nflow aloft, expect breezy conditions across the high terrain, \nparticularly northern Front Range and North Park where gusts of\n30-40 mph is possible throughout the afternoon. As of right now,\nit looks like we will hold on to enough moisture to keep relative\nhumidity values above critical fire weather thresholds, but will \nkeep monitoring for any drying trends.\n\nGuidance continues to be in good agreement of a pattern change\nby next week, although there is still some uncertainty in the \nforecast and we might not see that change until later next week. \nAn upper level trough will enter the Pacific Northwest, become a \nclosed low and travel south along California Sunday and Monday. \nWhile it looks like we will initially miss the colder air \nassociated with this system, 700-mb temperatures will begin to \ncool, resulting in slightly lower surface temperatures than what \nwe have been seeing by sometime Sunday/Monday. However, there is \nstill a spread in maximum temperatures both days, with latest \nensemble guidance trending warmer for Monday. General consensus \nseems to be somewhere in the 70s Sunday and Monday. In terms of \nprecipitation, we will likely stay dry across the plains and most \nof the high terrain as the bulk of the moisture from the remnants \nof Hurricane Rachel stays south and west of our region. \n\nBy mid-to-late next week, another upper level trough will enter\nthe Pacific Northwest and absorb the aforementioned upper level\nlow. This system looks to finally bring more seasonal temperatures\nthe forecast area. There is still some uncertainty with the exact\ntravel and evolution of this system, but model guidance has\ncontinued to hint at lower elevation precipitation by the end of\nnext week.",
